examples.solution_text:
The intercepts of $y=(x+2)(x-5)$ occur when either factor is zero: $x=-2$ or $x=5$. The positive coefficient of $x^{2}$ gives a concave-up parabola.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=2mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{solutionblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[<->,black] (-3.5,0)--(6.5,0);
\draw[->,black] (0,-14)--(0,9);
\path (6.5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[solutionblue,thick,<->,domain=-3:6,samples=65] plot (\x,{(\x+2)*(\x-5)});
\fill[black] (-2,0) circle (1.5pt) (5,0) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-2,0) node[above left] {$-2$} (5,0) node[above right] {$5$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is below the $x$-axis between the intercepts. The strict inequality excludes both endpoints.
$-2<x<5$, or $(-2,5)$.
For comparison, the same graph is positive outside the intercepts: $x<-2$ or $x>5$.

Factorise the quadratic:
$\begin{aligned}y&=x^{2}+x-12\\&=(x+4)(x-3)\end{aligned}$
The intercepts are $x=-4$ and $x=3$. The parabola is concave up.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=2mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{solutionblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[<->,black] (-5.5,0)--(4.5,0);
\draw[->,black] (0,-14)--(0,9);
\path (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[solutionblue,thick,<->,domain=-5:4,samples=65] plot (\x,{(\x+4)*(\x-3)});
\fill[black] (-4,0) circle (1.5pt) (3,0) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-4,0) node[above left] {$-4$} (3,0) node[above right] {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is on or above the $x$-axis outside the intercepts. Include both endpoints because equality is allowed.
$x\leq-4$ or $x\geq3$, or $(-\infty,-4]\cup[3,\infty)$.

Move all terms to the left:
$\begin{aligned}x^{2}-2x-15&\leq0\\(x-5)(x+3)&\leq0\end{aligned}$
Sketch $y=(x-5)(x+3)$. Its intercepts are $x=-3$ and $x=5$, and it is concave up.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6.5mm,y=1.7mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{solutionblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[<->,black] (-4.5,0)--(6.5,0);
\draw[->,black] (0,-18)--(0,10);
\path (6.5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,10) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[solutionblue,thick,<->,domain=-4:6,samples=65] plot (\x,{(\x-5)*(\x+3)});
\fill[black] (-3,0) circle (1.5pt) (5,0) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-3,0) node[above left] {$-3$} (5,0) node[above right] {$5$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is on or below the $x$-axis between and including the intercepts.
$-3\leq x\leq5$, or $[-3,5]$.

Write the quadratic in factored form:
$\begin{aligned}y&=-x^{2}+x+6\\&=-(x-3)(x+2)\end{aligned}$
The intercepts are $x=-2$ and $x=3$. The negative coefficient of $x^{2}$ gives a concave-down parabola.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{solutionblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[<->,black] (-3.5,0)--(4.5,0);
\draw[->,black] (0,-7)--(0,8);
\path (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[solutionblue,thick,<->,domain=-3:4,samples=65] plot (\x,{-(\x-3)*(\x+2)});
\fill[black] (-2,0) circle (1.5pt) (3,0) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-2,0) node[above left] {$-2$} (3,0) node[above right] {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is on or below the $x$-axis outside the intercepts. Include both endpoints.
$x\leq-2$ or $x\geq3$, or $(-\infty,-2]\cup[3,\infty)$.
The same graph is strictly positive between the intercepts: $-2<x<3$.

Factorise:
$\begin{aligned}y&=2x^{2}+3x-2\\&=(2x-1)(x+2)\end{aligned}$
Find the intercepts:
$\begin{aligned}2x-1&=0\\x&=\frac12\end{aligned}$
The other intercept is $x=-2$. Since $a=2>0$, the parabola is concave up.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=13mm,y=3mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{solutionblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[<->,black] (-3,0)--(1.5,0);
\draw[->,black] (0,-4.5)--(0,6);
\path (1.5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[solutionblue,thick,<->,domain=-2.7:1.2,samples=65] plot (\x,{(2*\x-1)*(\x+2)});
\fill[black] (-2,0) circle (1.5pt) (0.5,0) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-2,0) node[above left] {$-2$} (0.5,0) node[above right] {$\frac12$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph is above the $x$-axis outside the intercepts. Exclude both endpoints.
$x<-2$ or $x>\frac12$, or $(-\infty,-2)\cup(\frac12,\infty)$.
